  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...  \nThe more impactful weather is expected on Sunday as the remnants of \nRachel move through. According to the latest official forecast track \nfrom the National Hurricane Center, Rachel is expected to accelerate \nnorth-northeastward along the vicinity of the Lower Colorado River \nValley. An area of heavy rainfall is likely to follow along and just \nto the west of where Rachel tracks. Even though the official NHC \nforecast has the system tracking along the vicinity of the Lower \nColorado River Valley suggesting that the heavy rainfall may fall \nacross portions of southeast CA, there is a lot of spread amongst \nthe guidance in the track as some solutions have Rachel tracking \nnear the southern CA coast whereas other solutions have the system \ntracking as far east as southwest AZ. Therefore, the rainfall \nforecast is still highly volatile and could still significantly \nshift depending on the eventual track. The greater impact with \nRachel will be the potential for strong  winds as a belt of strong \n850 mb winds in excess of 40-50 kts is likely to set up along the \neastern quadrant of Rachel with much of the guidance showing gusts \nexceeding 40-50 mph across much of southwest into south-central AZ, \nespecially early Sunday morning through Sunday afternoon. Wind gusts \nthis high could certainly result in areas of blowing dust across the \ndust prone areas. Given the strong wind potential, a wind advisory \nhas been hoisted from eastern Riverside and Imperial Counties \nthrough all of southwest and south-central AZ. In addition to the \nwind threat, there will also be the potential along the eastern \nquadrant for multiple rounds of widely scattered showers and storms \nto materialize. There is a potential for some of these storms to \nreach severe levels given that strong vertical shear in excess of 50 \nkts will be present, however, the severe potential will be dependent \non how much instability will be present. \n\nEven though the remnants of Rachel will be well north of the area by \nSunday evening, unsettled conditions will continue right through \nearly next week as the main west coast trough moves through the \nregion bringing additional chances for showers and isolated \nthunderstorms, with the highest chances across the foothills and \nhigher terrain areas to the north and east of Phoenix from upslope \nflow. Thereafter, drier and quieter weather is expected by the \nmiddle of the week as the main trough axis lifts to the northeast. \n\nUnder the presence of the troughing pattern, well-below normal \ntemperatures are expected during the first half of next week with \nlower deserts highs going from the low to mid 80s on Sunday to \nmostly 70s Tuesday and Wednesday. Early morning lows will also take \na tumble with the coolest morning expected on Wednesday as \ntemperatures are expected to bottom out in the 50s across much of \nthe region with even some 40s across the higher terrain areas.",
